Draw the window frame of Plasma 6 with a KDecoration3 plugin

QtCurve draws no window border on a Plasma 5 session and none on a Plasma 6
session. The tree holds two decorations, qt5/kwin and qt6/kwin. Both include
kcommondecoration.h, which is a header of the decoration library of KDE4. The
CMakeLists.txt file of each one returns at once unless QTC_QT4_ENABLE_KWIN is
on. qt4/CMakeLists.txt turns that variable off for every build without Qt4, so
neither directory builds. A Plasma 6 user therefore gets the Breeze title bar
beside the QtCurve widget style.

This is upstream issue 5. The patch adds qt6/kdecoration, a plugin for
KDecoration3 6. KWin reads a decoration plugin from the org.kde.kdecoration3
directory of the Qt plugin path. The build installs the plugin there, beside
org.kde.breeze.so, under the name org.kde.qtcurve.so.

QtCurve::Decoration draws the title bar, the caption, the window border and the
drop shadow. QtCurve::Button draws one title bar button.
QtCurve::DecorationConfig reads the two configuration files.
QtCurve::ShadowConfig holds the drop shadow settings of one window state.

The build gets a new option, QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION. The option follows the
idiom of the project. The default is On for a build with QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE.
Every other build gets Off, because the plugin links KF6. A build without
KDecoration3 drops the decoration with a warning. A command line value of On
makes that miss a hard error.

How the plugin reads the configuration. The KDE4 decoration reached the
settings of the style through a QStyle. qtcurvehandler.cpp built the style with
QStyleFactory::create() and asked pixelMetric() for QtC_Round.
qtcurveclient.cpp and qtcurvebutton.cpp asked for QtC_TitleBarApp and
QtC_TitleBarButtons. KWin holds no QStyle for a decoration now, so that route
is gone.

The plugin compiles qt6/common/config_file.cpp into itself and calls
qtcReadConfig(), which is the call that the style makes in Style::init(). The
plugin therefore reads the file of the style, and the QTCURVE_CONFIG_FILE
environment variable still names that file. The settings dialogue of the style
drives the frame. The plugin also reads the kwinqtcurverc file of the KDE4
decoration. The KDE4 settings module wrote the border shades and the drop
shadow settings there.

The plugin reads both files one time, at the first decoration of the session.
KDecoration3 gives no reload signal for a file outside its own settings, so a
change of either file needs a restart of the compositor.

The plugin honours these keys of the file of the style:

* round. A value below the full value gives a square window. Every other value
  gives round corners. QtCurveClient::paintEvent() in
  qt6/kwin/qtcurveclient.cpp made the same test.
* titlebarAppearance and inactiveTitlebarAppearance. The appearance of the
  window state fills the title bar. A flat appearance gives a plain fill. Every
  other appearance gives the gradient of qtcGetGradient(). The top corner
  radius is 6.0 pixels for the no appearance value. It is 8.0 pixels for every
  other value. The KDE4 decoration used the same two values.
* titlebarButtonAppearance and sunkenAppearance. The first fills a button. The
  second fills a pressed button.
* titlebarButtons. The round flag gives a round button. The no frame flag drops
  the frame, and the hover frame flag drops it until the pointer arrives. The
  hover symbol flag fades the glyph, and the full hover symbol flag hides it.
  The colour flag, the inactive colour flag, the pointer colour flag and the
  symbol colour flag take the colours of titlebarButtonColors.
* titlebarButtonColors. The key gives a colour to each button type.
* windowBorder. The light border flag draws the light line inside the top
  border. The title bar colour flag gives the border the window background, and
  the border keeps the title bar colour while the flag is off. The separator
  flag draws a line under the title bar of an active window. The fill title bar
  flag adds the window background under the title bar appearance. The
  appearance fills the title bar while the flag is off as well.
* titlebarAlignment, titlebarIcon and titlebarEffect. The first places the
  caption. The second drops the window icon for the no icon value, and keeps
  the icon beside the caption for the other two values. The third draws a
  second caption under the caption. The colour for the shadow value is a ten
  percent black mix of the title bar colour. The colour for the etch value is a
  sixty percent white mix of the same colour.
* contrast, darkerBorders, customShades and shading. The four keys build the
  shade table. shadeColors() in qt6/style/qtcurve.cpp builds the same table.
  The border line takes entry 5 and the light line takes entry 0.

The plugin honours these keys of the kwinqtcurverc file:

* OuterBorder and InnerBorder of the General group. The two keys change the
  border width the way setBorderSize() in qt6/kwin/qtcurvehandler.cpp changed
  it. A window with no outer border loses one pixel of a border wider than the
  normal width. A window with both borders and a small border size gains two
  pixels. The two keys also give the colour of the outer line and of the inner
  line. A key selects the light shade, the border shade, or the colour of the
  drop shadow. The no shade value keeps the border shade for the outer line,
  so a window always keeps a visible edge.
* RoundBottom, TitleBarPad and EdgePad of the General group. The first selects
  a bottom corner radius of 6.0 pixels or 1.0 pixel. The second adds two times
  its value to the title bar height. The floor of the height applies after that
  addition. The third adds its value to the side border and to the bottom
  border.
* CustomShadows of the General group, and the Size, HOffset, VOffset, ColorType
  and Color keys of the ActiveShadows group and of the InactiveShadows group.
  QtCurve::ShadowConfig is a port of qt6/kwin/qtcurveshadowconfiguration.cpp,
  which carries no MIT notice. The decoration builds the shadow tiles with
  qtcShadowCreate() of lib/utils/shadow.cpp, as qt6/style/shadowhelper.cpp does
  after the Wayland shadow patch. An offset makes the padding of one side
  larger and the padding of the other side smaller, which moves the whole
  shadow. A false CustomShadows value gives every window the fixed grey shadow
  of the QtCurve menus, which is the shadow of qtcX11ShadowInit() in
  lib/utils/x11helpers.cpp.

These settings stay without effect. The header states them, so a reader does
not look for them:

* The BorderSize key of the kwinqtcurverc file. The border width follows the
  border size of KDecoration3. The settings module of Plasma 6 owns that value,
  and the KDE4 settings module no longer runs. The width table of
  setBorderSize() gives 2 pixels for Tiny, 4 for Normal, 8 for Large, then 12,
  18, 27 and 40. KDecoration3 gives the None size and the NoSides size a
  different meaning, so the plugin drops the row of 1 pixel. None gives no
  border. NoSides gives no side border and a bottom border of the normal
  width.
* The blend title bar flag and the menu bar colour flag of windowBorder. Both
  need the menu bar of the client window, and a decoration plugin cannot reach
  it.
* menubarHiding and statusbarHiding. The KDE4 decoration toggled the two bars
  through a D-Bus call from its own title bar menu, and the plugin has no such
  menu.
* ActiveOpacity, InactiveOpacity, OpaqueBorder, BorderlessMax and Grouping of
  the kwinqtcurverc file. KWin owns the window opacity, the borderless state
  and the window grouping in Plasma 6.
* A settings module for the decoration. This round adds no module, so every
  value of the two lists above comes from the two files by hand.

A maximized axis and a screen edge each get no border. A maximized axis also
gets no resize grab area, because the area falls outside the screen. The title
bar takes every click inside the top border, so the top edge gets a resize grab
area of the large spacing of KDecoration3. A side edge and the bottom edge get
that area only where the border size gives no border there. The None size
therefore gives the grab area to all three edges, and the NoSides size gives it
to the two sides. The Breeze decoration makes the same choice.

The plugin also reports a corner radius, and KWin cuts the window to it. The
two top corners get the radius of the title bar. The two bottom corners get the
bottom radius only where the bottom border is zero, because the window content
reaches the corner there. A maximized axis and a screen edge each give a
straight edge, and a straight edge gets a radius of zero.

The title bar height is the caption font height plus 4 pixels and two times the
TitleBarPad value. A negative pad value drives the sum down, so the floor of 16
pixels applies after the pad. readConfig() in qt6/kwin/qtcurvehandler.cpp uses
the same figure, but it applies the floor before the pad. A negative pad
therefore drives that title bar below 16 pixels. The height is odd, so a button
and a glyph both keep a centre pixel.

A window at a screen edge keeps no gap between the outermost title bar button
and that edge. The button grows over the gap and takes every click up to the
edge, which the pointer reaches without aim. The button at the top edge grows
over the gap above it as well. The frame and the glyph keep the square of the
button, so the look stays the same. The Breeze decoration makes the same
choice.

KDecoration3 puts the buttons of a group from the right under a right to left
layout. The plugin reads the layout direction and grows the button at the outer
end of the group. A button that the window state hides keeps no place in the
layout, so the plugin drops it from that choice. The plugin drops a Spacer
button and a Custom button as well. KDecoration3 disables a Spacer, so a Spacer
takes no click. The plugin paints neither type.

The left group starts on its anchor. The right group starts one box width
inside the right anchor. The group box keeps the spacing of a hidden button at
the end of the list. The box is then wider than the buttons in it, and the
button at the outer end stops short of the anchor. The plugin therefore moves
the whole group, until that button sits on the anchor. A Spacer at the outer
end of the list moves past the anchor, and it paints nothing there.

A button can also hide while the window lives. The plugin hides the Close, Maximize, Minimize, ContextHelp and Shade
buttons in Button::create() when the window drops the ability. KDecoration3
hides the ApplicationMenu, OnAllDesktops and ExcludeFromCapture buttons itself.
The plugin follows the visibility signal of each button, so the growth and the
group position stay with the visible buttons.

Facts. A headless harness loads a decoration plugin with KPluginFactory. It
builds the decoration with the mock bridge of the KDecoration3 test suite. The
harness sizes the window 520 by 140 pixels. It fills a QImage with 250, 250,
250, and then paints the decoration over that fill.

The mock window of the test suite gives an empty caption, no window colour and
no active state. The harness adds the caption "QtCurve on Plasma 6" and an
active state. It adds a title bar colour of 214, 218, 224. It adds a window
frame colour of 220, 223, 227. It adds a caption colour of 35, 38, 39.

The harness also takes the border size of the mock settings from an environment
variable. The size is Normal where the variable is absent.

* With no configuration file the decoration reports borders of 4, 23, 4 and 4
  pixels, and a size of 528 by 167 pixels. The resize grab margins are 0, 12, 0
  and 0 pixels. The corner radius values are 8, 8, 0 and 0 pixels. The pixel at
  264, 2 is 246, 247, 248. The pixel at 264, 20 is 214, 218, 224. The gradient
  therefore runs from a light top to the plain title bar colour. The shadow
  image is 69 by 69 pixels with a padding of 29 pixels on each side. The
  strongest shadow pixel is grey 101, 101, 101.
* A file with titlebarAppearance=flat and inactiveTitlebarAppearance=flat moves
  the pixel at 264, 2 to 214, 218, 224. A flat appearance gives the plain title
  bar colour. A file with shinyglass in the two keys moves the pixel at 264, 22
  to 227, 230, 234. A third appearance therefore gives a third title bar.
* A file with round=slight moves the four corner radius values to zero. It also
  moves the pixel at 0, 0 from 250, 250, 250 to 179, 185, 194. The first value
  is the fill behind the round corner. The second value is the border line of a
  square corner.
* The None border size gives borders of 0, 23, 0 and 0 pixels. It gives grab
  margins of 12 pixels on each side. It gives corner radius values of 8, 8, 6
  and 6 pixels. The NoSides border size gives borders of 0, 23, 0 and 4 pixels.
  It gives grab margins of 12, 12, 12 and 0 pixels. It gives corner radius
  values of 8, 8, 0 and 0 pixels.
* A kwinqtcurverc file with TitleBarPad=-5 moves the borders to 4, 17, 4 and 4
  pixels. The floor of 16 pixels holds, and the height stays odd.
* A maximized window of 520 pixels reports borders of 0, 23, 0 and 0 pixels.
  The leftmost button takes the pixel at 0, 0. The rightmost button takes the
  pixel at 519, 0. Each button still paints its frame and its glyph inside a
  square of 17 pixels.
* A right to left layout gives the same two pixels to the two outermost
  buttons. It also keeps the gap of 2 pixels between two squares. A window that
  hides the close button keeps the same two pixels under each layout direction.
* A maximized window of 520 pixels hides a title bar button after the first
  layout. A left to right layout keeps a button on the pixel at 519, 0. A
  right to left layout keeps a button on the pixel at 0, 0. The result holds
  for the close button at the end of the group and for the maximize button in
  the middle. It also holds after a change of the button list, which builds
  new buttons.
* A maximized window of 520 pixels holds a Spacer at each end of each group.
  The menu button takes the pixel at 0, 0 and the close button takes the pixel
  at 519, 0. The Spacer of the right group starts at 522 pixels, which lies
  outside the window. A right to left layout gives the pixel at 0, 0 to the
  close button and the pixel at 519, 0 to the menu button.
* A kwinqtcurverc file moves the borders to 8, 29, 8 and 8 pixels. The General
  group holds OuterBorder=2, InnerBorder=1, TitleBarPad=3, EdgePad=2 and
  CustomShadows=true. The ActiveShadows group holds Size=45, HOffset=4,
  VOffset=8, ColorType=5 and Color=255,0,0. The shadow image grows to 99 by 99
  pixels. The padding becomes 40, 36, 48 and 52 pixels, and the strongest
  shadow pixel becomes 255, 0, 0.

Drop this patch when QtCurve ships a KDecoration3 decoration upstream.

diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
index 347c7c90..22230ccb 100644
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -46,6 +46,14 @@ qtc_option(ENABLE_QT5 "Building Qt5 theme." On)
 qtc_option(ENABLE_QT6 "Building Qt6 theme." On)
 qtc_option(ENABLE_GTK2 "Building Gtk2 theme." On)
 qtc_option(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE "Building Qt6 style with KDE Frameworks 6 support." Off)
+# The Plasma 6 window decoration links KF6, so it needs QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE.
+if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE)
+  qtc_option(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION
+    "Building the Plasma 6 window decoration." On)
+else()
+  qtc_option(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION
+    "Building the Plasma 6 window decoration." Off)
+endif()
 # option(ENABLE_GTK3 "Building Gtk3 theme." On)
 option(BUILD_TESTING "Enable testing." On)
 option(QTC_ENABLE_X11 "Enable X11" On)
diff --git a/qt6/CMakeLists.txt b/qt6/CMakeLists.txt
index 31f31ba6..38e135ac 100644
--- a/qt6/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/qt6/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -63,6 +63,25 @@ if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE)
   if(NOT APPLE)
     set(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KWIN ON)
   endif()
+else()
+  # The decoration links KF6, so a build without KDE support drops it.
+  set(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION Off)
+endif()
+
+# The Plasma 6 window decoration is a KDecoration3 plugin. A build that asks
+# for the decoration on the command line fails when KDecoration3 is absent.
+# Every other build drops the decoration and keeps going.
+if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION)
+  find_package(KDecoration3 QUIET)
+  if(NOT KDecoration3_FOUND)
+    if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION_DEFINED)
+      message(FATAL_ERROR "QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION is on, but "
+        "find_package(KDecoration3) found nothing.")
+    endif()
+    message(WARNING "KDecoration3 is absent. The build drops the Plasma 6 "
+      "window decoration.")
+    set(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION Off)
+  endif()
 endif()
 
 add_subdirectory(common)
@@ -71,4 +90,7 @@ if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KWIN)
   add_subdirectory(kwin)
   add_subdirectory(kwinconfig)
 endif()
+if(QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDECORATION)
+  add_subdirectory(kdecoration)
+endif()
 add_subdirectory(style)
diff --git a/qt6/kdecoration/CMakeLists.txt b/qt6/kdecoration/CMakeLists.txt
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..e4c868ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/qt6/kdecoration/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+set(qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS
+  qtcurvedecoration.cpp
+  qtcurvedecorationbutton.cpp
+  qtcurvedecorationconfig.cpp)
+set(qtcurve_kdecoration_HDRS
+  qtcurvedecoration.h
+  qtcurvedecorationbutton.h
+  qtcurvedecorationconfig.h)
+
+translate_add_sources(${qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS} ${qtcurve_kdecoration_HDRS}
+  qtcurve.json)
+
+# The decoration reads the configuration file of the QtCurve style, so it
+# compiles the reader of the style into the plugin, as qt6/style does.
+set(qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS ${qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS}
+  ../common/common.cpp
+  ../common/config_file.cpp)
+
+# The decoration makes its own drop shadow tiles. lib/utils keeps
+# qtcShadowCreate() hidden, so the decoration compiles the tile generator into
+# the plugin, as qt6/style does.
+set(qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS ${qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS}
+  ../../lib/utils/shadow.cpp)
+
+# The shadow colour of the ActiveShadows group follows the colour scheme of the
+# session, so the plugin needs KColorScheme. The style needs no such component.
+find_package(KF6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS ColorScheme)
+
+include_directories(
+  "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}"
+  "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}")
+
+add_library(qtcurve-kdecoration MODULE ${qtcurve_kdecoration_SRCS})
+# KDecoration3/decoration.h declares a defaulted three way comparison, so the
+# headers need C++20. The rest of QtCurve builds with C++17.
+set_target_properties(qtcurve-kdecoration PROPERTIES
+  OUTPUT_NAME "org.kde.qtcurve"
+  LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}"
+  PREFIX "")
+target_compile_options(qtcurve-kdecoration PRIVATE -std=c++20)
+target_link_libraries(qtcurve-kdecoration
+  Qt6::Core
+  Qt6::Gui
+  Qt6::Widgets
+  Qt6::Svg
+  KF6::ColorScheme
+  KF6::ConfigCore
+  KF6::CoreAddons
+  KF6::GuiAddons
+  KDecoration3::KDecoration
+  qtcurve-utils)
+
+# KWin looks for a decoration plugin in the org.kde.kdecoration3 directory of
+# the Qt plugin path.
+install(TARGETS qtcurve-kdecoration LIBRARY
+  DESTINATION "${_Qt6_PLUGIN_INSTALL_DIR}/${KDECORATION_PLUGIN_DIR}")
